## 5. PRODUCT JUDGEMENT CALLS

Every one of these is a decision I made that you might reasonably make differently. None of
them is hidden in the code.

### 5.1 An assigned flow still beats a CRM binding

The hook fires only for a DID with **no flow assigned**. If you bind a number that also has
a flow, the flow runs and the CRM link does nothing for that call.

`docs/DEFAULT_CALL_HANDLING_SPEC.md` already establishes that an assigned flow overrides the
built-in default handling, and a CRM binding is a *different default*, not a higher
authority. Putting the hook above the flow resolution would have meant a CRM binding silently
overriding a call flow somebody had deliberately built — exactly the class of surprise the
"do not change what works" constraint is about.

**Consequence:** a bound DID that also has a flow gets no CRM events. If you want the ring
group on a flow-assigned number, unassign the flow (or tell me and I will add an explicit
`crm_links.overrides_flow` column, which is the honest way to express "yes, I mean it").

### 5.2 The report job posts to OWEN, which posts to the CRM

This is the least obvious design decision in the module, and it is driven by a fact I found
in the compose files rather than by taste.

`handle_crm_report` runs in the **worker** container. `docker-compose.prod.yml` puts `worker`
on `callmon-net` **only** — not on `traefik-public`, which is the network `callmon_app` and
`ghl_clone_api` share. **The worker cannot resolve `ghl_clone_api` at all.**

The two ways out were (a) add `traefik-public` to the worker's networks, or (b) hop through
the app container, which is on both. I chose (b), for two reasons:

1. It needs **no change to `docker-compose.prod.yml`**, which is deployment topology on a
   live system.
2. A job payload is a row in the `jobs` table. Putting a `ghl_pat_...` bearer in its
   `headers` would persist a live CRM credential in Postgres, where `pg_dump` and the
   `owen_ro` role behind `/api/ai/query` can both read it. Resolving the token at the
   app-side adapter keeps it in the app container's environment.

This is also exactly the hop `flows/runtime.py::_enqueue_crm_report` already uses for the
built-in `kind: ghl` adapter (`OWEN_INTERNAL_URL` + `X-OWEN-Key`), so it is an established
seam rather than one I invented.

### 5.3 Three lifecycle events, but only ONE of them is a `CALL`

You asked for call started / answered / ended. All three are sent. But:

* `started` and `answered` are `type: INTERNAL_COMMENT`, `direction: OUTBOUND`
* `ended` is `type: CALL` with the real duration, direction and `call_status`

**Why.** The CRM's `automations.on_inbound_call` fires the **missed-call auto-text-back** for
any INBOUND `CALL` row whose `duration_seconds` is ≤ 15 or absent. Sending "call started" as
a `CALL` row would tell the CRM every single call was missed and queue a text to the customer
**while they were still on the phone**. `INTERNAL_COMMENT` is in the CRM's `INTERNAL_TYPES`
set: recorded on the thread, never transmitted, no automation. `OUTBOUND` so a machine note
does not bump the thread's unread badge.

Everything the CRM's schema has no field for — which of the three phones answered, the
recording id, the transcript id, the `owen_call_id` — is in the event's `body` text, because
`ConversationEvent` has exactly four typed slots and none of them is "winning destination".

(In the CRM's v1 the outbound transport is `LoggingTransport`, so that text-back is currently
recorded as intent and nothing is transmitted. That is the CRM's decision to reverse, not
ours, so I built this as though the text were real.)

### 5.4 PSTN legs present the BOUND DID, not the caller's number

**This is the reason the hybrid ring is a new function and not a call into `ring_and_bridge`.**

`ring_and_bridge` applies ONE `callerId` to every leg. The two leg kinds need different ones:

* An **operator** leg wants `"<dialed DID>" <caller number>`, because the browser popup parses
  that string — display name is the number that was dialled, URI user is who is calling
  (`DEFAULT_CALL_HANDLING_SPEC`, "How the popup learns to what number"). My module builds that
  string byte-identically, so the popup keeps working.
* A **PSTN** leg must NOT present the inbound caller's number. Sending a number we do not own
  out over the BulkVS trunk is caller-ID spoofing: the carrier may reject the INVITE, and
  where it does not, a callback from the mobile that rang goes to the *customer* instead of to
  the business.

So a PSTN leg presents `"<caller number>" <bound DID>` — the bound DID as the number, the
caller's number as the display name. The display name is a free bet (most PSTN carriers drop
it); where it survives, the mobile shows both who is calling and a working callback number.

**The trade-off you should know about:** if the display name is dropped, whoever answers their
cell sees the business's own number and cannot tell who is calling until they pick up. If you
would rather have caller-ID passthrough on PSTN legs and accept the spoofing risk, it is one
function — `ring.pstn_caller_id` — and one test.

### 5.5 Contacts are never auto-created in the CRM

`POST /api/events` requires a `contact_id` and 404s on anything else. A caller who is not
already in the CRM has nowhere to land, and I do **not** create them. The event is dropped
with a logged reason and the job **completes** (not retried — an unknown caller will not
become known on the sixth attempt).

`docs/CRM_CONTEXT_SPEC.md` C10 records the "hundreds of junk contacts" failure and
`WORKIZ_IMPORT.md` records how painful GHL cleanup is. Auto-creating a contact per inbound
call, from a week-old integration, is how you get there. If you want it, it should be its own
decision with its own rules (spam filtering, minimum duration), not a side effect of logging.

**Consequence:** until a caller exists in the CRM, their calls are not reported. You will see
`crm-link: dropping ended event for call <id> — no CRM contact matches +1...` at WARNING,
which surfaces in `GET /api/ai/errors`.

### 5.6 Contact matching is EXACT on the last ten digits, never fuzzy

The CRM's contact search is a substring ILIKE and will happily return a different customer
whose number contains the same seven digits. Every candidate is confirmed by comparing the
last ten digits. Filing a call on the wrong customer's timeline is worse than filing it
nowhere — the judgement C3 already made about greeting the wrong person by name.

### 5.7 `bind` creates the row DISABLED

`manage bind` writes `enabled=false` and tells you the command to turn it on. Binding a
number that rings in a real business should take two deliberate acts, with a `manage list` in
between.

### 5.8 The allowlist is a permission, the binding is an intent

`crm_links.pstn_numbers` says what a DID *should* ring. `CRM_LINK_ALLOWLIST` says what may
*actually* be dialled. They are checked separately, at call time, every call. So a number
configured on a binding but missing from the allowlist is stored, shown in `manage list` as
`REFUSED`, warned about at bind time — and never rung.

An **empty allowlist allows nothing**, including numbers on a binding. That is deliberate:
while this is new, the failure mode of a forgotten or mis-typed allowlist has to be "no call
went out".

### 5.9 The `crm_links` table, not columns on `numbers`

`numbers` is written by the BulkVS sync and read by ingestion, billing, the Inbox and the flow
runtime. Integration-specific columns there would put this module inside the blast radius of
all of them and make "turn the integration off" a schema question. A separate table with an FK
is removable in one migration and invisible to anything that does not join to it.

### 5.10 Secrets are named, not stored

`crm_links.crm_token_env` holds the **name** of an environment variable. A `ghl_pat_...` in a
column would be readable by the `owen_ro` role behind `/api/ai/query` and would ride into
every `pg_dump`.

